        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 md:p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The best protein supplement is the one that fills the gap in your diet at the lowest cost per useful gram. For most people that means a large tub of whey, or a certified plant blend if dairy is off the table, with clear whey as an option if milky shakes put you off. Everything else is a convenience purchase: bottled shakes for days away from a kitchen, bars for bags and travel.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Work out cost per 25 g before you buy, read the protein sources rather than the front of the pack, and do not count collagen as if it were whey. Supplements help most when the rest of your diet and training are already in place; they cannot do the work for you, but chosen well they make the work easier.
          </p>
